adjudication.rationale:
The diff adds a new query in `scheduleEmailReminders.ts`:
```ts
const remindersToCancel = await prisma.workflowReminder.findMany({
  where: { cancelled: true, scheduledDate: { lte: dayjs().add(1, "hour").toISOString() } },
});
```
This is an unbounded `findMany` (no `take`/pagination) and returns full `WorkflowReminder` records (no `select`), so it can materialize an arbitrary number of rows each run. The migration/schema changes in the diff only add `cancelled Boolean?` and do not add any index in this change, so the performance concern introduced by this query is diff-grounded (even if an index might exist elsewhere, none is added here).

In `deleteScheduledSMSReminder` (smsReminderManager.ts), the Prisma delete is executed after `twilio.cancelSMS(referenceId)` within the same `try` block:

```ts
if (referenceId) {
  await twilio.cancelSMS(referenceId);
}
await prisma.workflowReminder.delete(...);
```

If `twilio.cancelSMS` throws (e.g., not cancelable/already sent), control jumps to `catch` and the DB row is not deleted. In the previous flow shown elsewhere in the diff, DB deletion was done separately from the external cancel call (call sites used `prisma.workflowReminder.deleteMany(...)` regardless), so this change can leave `WorkflowReminder` rows behind unintentionally.

In `handleNewBooking.ts` the new reschedule cancellation block calls async functions inside `forEach` without `await`/`Promise.all`:
```ts
originalRescheduledBooking.workflowReminders.forEach((reminder) => {
  deleteScheduledEmailReminder(...);
  deleteScheduledSMSReminder(...);
});
```
Both helpers are `async` (see `emailReminderManager.ts` / `smsReminderManager.ts`), so this launches promises that are not awaited. The surrounding `try/catch` cannot catch async rejections from those calls, and the handler may proceed/finish before cancellations complete (especially relevant in API/serverless execution), leading to reminders not being reliably cancelled.
